The set represented by an ideal element belongs to the family.

It is the union of the associated family of closures. That family is nonempty because it contains the closure of the empty token. Each member is the closure of a token and therefore belongs to the original family. The family of closures is directed under inclusion, because two such closures are dominated by the closure of a token supplied by the directedness step. The directed-union hypothesis places the union in the original family.
